 
What is Sidecar Container?

	A Sidecar container runs along with the main container in the same Pod.

	👉 It runs continuously and supports the main container.

🎯 Why we use Sidecar?

	Logging (e.g., log collector)

	Monitoring (Prometheus exporters)

	Proxy (service mesh like Istio)

	File sync or config reload


👉 Both containers:

	Start together

	Share network & storage

	Run simultaneously


Example: 

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: sidecar-demo
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    volumeMounts:
    - name: shared-data
      mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html

  - name: sidecar
    image: busybox
    command: ['sh', '-c', 'while true; do echo Hello > /data/index.html; sleep 5; done']
    volumeMounts:
    - name: shared-data
      mountPath: /data

  volumes:
  - name: shared-data
    emptyDir: {}


 🧪 How to Test Sidecar

1. Deploy pod

	kubectl apply -f sidecar.yaml

2. Check running containers

	kubectl get pods
	kubectl describe pod sidecar-demo

3. Check logs (sidecar container)

	kubectl logs sidecar-demo -c sidecar

4. Exec into main container

	kubectl get all -n dev   take pod name and add in 

	kubectl exec -it <podname> -n dev -c writer-sidecar -- /bin/sh

5. Verify shared file

	cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html



Key Differences

Feature			Init Container					Sidecar Container

Execution		Runs before app						Runs with app

Lifecycle		Runs once									Runs continuously 

Purpose			Setup / initialization		Support main container

Dependency	Must complete								Independent

Example			DB check, migrations				Logging, proxy 